The easiest and most efficient way to change the color of a `<span>` on hover is using **CSS**. However, you can also do this using **JavaScript** if you need dynamic behavior. 

Here are the three best ways to do this:

---

### Method 1: Using CSS (Recommended)
This is the cleanest and most performance-friendly method. You use the `:hover` pseudo-class in your stylesheet.

**HTML:**
```html
<span class="hover-color">Hover over me!</span>
```

**CSS:**
```css
/* Default color */
.hover-color {
  color: black;
  transition: color 0.3s ease; /* Optional: Makes the color change smooth */
}

/* Color on mouse over */
.hover-color:hover {
  color: blue; 
  cursor: pointer; /* Optional: Changes cursor to a pointer finger */
}
```

---

### Method 2: Inline HTML & JavaScript (For quick, simple fixes)
If you cannot edit your external CSS file, you can use the inline `onmouseover` and `onmouseout` attributes directly inside your HTML tag.

**HTML:**
```html
<span 
  style="color: black;" 
  onmouseover="this.style.color='red'" 
  onmouseout="this.style.color='black'">
  Hover over me!
</span>
```

---

### Method 3: Using JavaScript Event Listeners (For dynamic JS apps)
If you are writing complex JavaScript and want to separate your logic from your HTML structure, you can add event listeners to the element.

**HTML:**
```html
<span id="my-span">Hover over me!</span>
```

**JavaScript:**
```javascript
const spanElement = document.getElementById('my-span');

// Change color when mouse enters
spanElement.addEventListener('mouseover', () => {
  spanElement.style.color = 'green';
});

// Change color back when mouse leaves
spanElement.addEventListener('mouseout', () => {
  spanElement.style.color = ''; // Resets to default CSS color
});
```

### Which one should you choose?
* Use **Method 1 (CSS)** 99% of the time because it keeps your styling separate from your logic and has the best performance.
* Use **Method 2** only for extremely quick testing or single-page emails where external stylesheets might not load.
* Use **Method 3** if the hover effect needs to trigger other complex JavaScript actions at the same time.

